<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00593</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>835d5ff1-db31-4df3-bbcd-528798e8f705</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For 1 - 4, use the following passage:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Harrison works two part time jobs. One at a gas station that pays $11 an hour and the other is IT troubleshooting for $16.50 an hour. Between the two jobs, Harrison wants to earn at least $330 a week. How many hours does Harrison need to work at each job to earn at least $330?</para>
</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="f9445750-3ace-41f1-9c19-cfe1e0882edc" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Enter Questions Below this line-->
  <!--q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="26e22785-9da2-41bd-817f-c237694558f1" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(11x + 16.5y ≥ 330\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(11x + 16.5y ≥ 330\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(16.5x + 11y ≥ 330\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(11x + 16.5y ≤ 330\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(16.5y + 11x &gt; 330\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>1. Let x be the number of hours he works at the gas station and let y be the number of hours he works troubleshooting. Which inequality correctly models this situation? </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! He makes 11x dollars at the gas station and \(16.5y\) dollars troubleshooting. The sum must be greater than or equal to 330 dollars.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="72ff8696-a5af-4446-98e7-adb762dc606a" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="c" data-solution-options="[&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;, &quot;d&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>2. Which graph correctly represents the inequality from the previous question? </para>
      <para>a.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 10 and the \(x\)-axis at 15. The region above the line is shaded.&#10;&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/294638ace54307c8532ae9b7e61b7592e5658bff"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>b.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 30 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/87e1101b4522dee5e1e0993a25a52826195a1fe8"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>c.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 20 and the \(x\)-axis at 30. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cac6f8950a8494af2e45b865c8f0680e1d5dec1b"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>d.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 30 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b83cde708f1c213773bc1c086f6b6216de8b9b07"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 20 and the \(x\)-axis at 30. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cac6f8950a8494af2e45b865c8f0680e1d5dec1b"/></media></figure></para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d28ed77f-752b-4446-b08e-44cc2b64267f" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(10, 20)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(10, 20)&quot;, &quot;(25, 2)&quot;, &quot;(15, 9)&quot;, &quot;(3, 17)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>3. Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the inequality?</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(11(10) + 16.5(20) = 440\), which is greater than 330</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b1d49162-ffed-4194-8ad1-c28d4588ff57" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="The solution represents a work schedule that earns him more than $330 per week." data-solution-options="[&quot;The y-coordinate represents how many hours Harrison will work at the gas station.&quot;, &quot;The x-coordinate represents how many hours he works at the IT job.&quot;, &quot;The solution represents a work schedule that earns him more than $330 per week.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>4. Which of the following is true about the solution you found in the previous question?</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The solution represents a work schedule where he earns 11x dollars at the gas station and \(16.5y \) dollars troubleshooting to earn more than $330 per week.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

<para>
  For 5 - 8, use the following passage:</para>
<blockquote>The doctor tells Maya she needs to exercise enough to burn 500 calories each day. She likes to run which burns 15 calories per minute or bike which burns 10 calories per minute.</blockquote>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="fa8ee198-68cf-40a7-a2fd-57beb8fa949f" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--MC-->
  <exercise data-content-id="97fccdef-959e-4130-8142-8133b6bd32aa" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(15x + 10y ≥ 500\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(15x + 10y &lt; 500\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(15x + 10y ≥ 500\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(15x + 10y &gt; 500\\)&quot;, &quot;15x + 10y ≤ 500&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>5. If x is the number of minutes that Maya runs and y is the number of minutes she bikes, find the inequality that models the situation.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! She burns 15x calories running and \(10y\) calories biking. The total calories must be greater than or equal to 500.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8e39b40d-f7f7-49fe-b845-0e77b6694c70" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="c" data-solution-options="[&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;, &quot;d&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>6. Which graph correctly represents the inequality from the previous question?</para>
      <para>a.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 25 and the \(x\)-axis at 35. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/67cef90769a04988a386016df6355ca380527017"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>b.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at 20. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f74cf13af60e3b751b57399cd5429407af3dc3b"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>c.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at about 33. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aa4147c08db38611cf8a1b18d1a2cef2c8280e61"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>d.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at about 33 and the \(x\)-axis at 50. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/604ed5ed1c5194bd9ba02f87b233c524d0317b25"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! <figure><media alt="A graph is shown of a line crossing the \(y\)-axis at 50 and the \(x\)-axis at about 33. The region above the line is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aa4147c08db38611cf8a1b18d1a2cef2c8280e61"/></media></figure></para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="31d8c372-5855-4b11-ae98-f692906a6709" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(15, 30)" data-solution-options="[&quot;(5, 40)&quot;, &quot;(15, 30)&quot;, &quot;(20, 15)&quot;, &quot;(25, 10)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>7. Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the inequality?</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(15(15) + 10(30) = 525\), which is greater than 500.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a1edcba0-490d-476a-b91f-6377952ab355" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="The solution represents a workout which allows Maya to burn at least 500 calories." data-solution-options="[&quot;The x-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Maya bikes.&quot;, &quot;The y-coordinate represents the number of minutes that Maya runs.&quot;, &quot;The solution represents a workout which allows Maya to burn at least 500 calories.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>8. Which of the following is true about the solution you found in the previous question?</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The solution represents a workout where she burns \(15x\) calories running and 10y calories biking to burn more than 500 calories.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.12.2 and 2.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
